## Report
Validated the finding. This is a logic/privacy bug rather than a memory-safety crash. I built and ran a targeted Go PoC test against the real appcore package. Evidence: current src/internal/appcore/autocapture.go:1118 enters the Discord upload block via autoCaptureDiscordUploadEnabled(r.Config); lines 1119-1122 prefer cfg.AutoCapture.Discord.WebhookURL before falling back to the primary r.Config.Discord.WebhookURL; lines 1127-1130 post either image or content; and lines 1154-1155 define the gate as cfg.AutoCapture.Discord.Enabled || cfg.Output.UploadDiscord. The parent commit used only cfg.Discord.Enabled, confirming the new global OR behavior was introduced. The actual capture flows call finalizeAutoCaptureImage from both photo and stream capture paths at src/internal/appcore/autocapture.go:837 and :965. The frontend also documents the changed behavior at src/frontend/src/main.js:2694-2700: normal Discord posting OR the auto-capture switch causes auto-captured images to be posted. Dynamic PoC: I added src/internal/appcore/autocapture_privacy_poc_test.go. TestPoCGlobalOutputEnablesAutoCaptureUploadDespiteDisabledAutoCaptureDiscord sets autoCapture.discord.enabled=false and output.uploadDiscord=true. It receives a Discord URL validation error, proving finalizeAutoCaptureImage entered the Discord posting path despite the auto-capture-specific flag being disabled. TestPoCDisabledAutoCaptureWebhookTakesPrecedenceWhenGloballyEnabled additionally sets a non-empty disabled autoCapture.discord.webhookUrl and a different primary webhook; the observed error corresponds to the disabled auto-capture webhook, proving that field is preferred once the global flag opens the path. Command output: `go test ./internal/appcore -run 'TestPoC' -count=1 -v` passed and logged: `first PoC result.Error="Discord Webhook URLは https:// で始まるURLを指定してください。"` and `second PoC result.Error="Discord Webhook URLは https:// で始まるURLを指定してください。"`. I also compiled a debug test binary with `go test ./internal/appcore -run 'TestPoC' -count=1 -gcflags='all=-N -l' -c -o /tmp/appcore_poc.test` and ran it successfully. Crash validation was not applicable because the issue is a consent-bypass/information-disclosure logic flaw; the debug binary did not crash. Valgrind and debugger attempts were made but unavailable in the container: `bash: command not found: valgrind`, `bash: command not found: gdb`, and `bash: command not found: dlv`.

